// tvbox: let the box user reboot and shut the box down from the power menu.
//
// Two actions, named exactly. `halt` is deliberately absent: it stops the machine
// without cutting power, which on a headless TV box is indistinguishable from a
// hang.
//
// The `-multiple-sessions` and `-ignore-inhibit` variants are left out too, and it
// is worth being precise about what that costs, because logind does NOT fall back
// to the plain action - it picks exactly one: with another uid's session present it
// checks only `...reboot-multiple-sessions`, with a block-mode shutdown inhibitor
// only `...reboot-ignore-inhibit`, and the plain `reboot` only when neither holds.
// So in those two cases the power menu is DENIED rather than merely delayed. Both
// are narrow here (a second uid logged in over SSH while someone presses Restart on
// the TV; a block-mode shutdown inhibitor, which nothing on this box takes -
// unattended-upgrades takes a delay lock and Chromium's blockers are idle/sleep),
// and the failure is now visible on screen rather than silent. Widen it here if a
// real box ever hits it.
//
// Matches on the GROUP rather than on `subject.active`, because the shell has no
// logind session at all: Electron moves its main process into its own systemd app
// scope, so the seat's session does not contain it and `subject.active` is false.
// This is the same trap the udisks grant documents, and the reason logind's own
// "an active local session may shut down" default never applied here.
//
// `video` is the group every tvbox user is in - provision.sh and the image both put
// it there - which is why it is the one that works on a flashed box as well as a
// dev-deployed one. Be honest about what it is not: it is a device-access group,
// not "the user that drives the screen", and this rule tests neither `subject.local`
// nor `subject.active`, so any member can also power the box off over SSH without
// authenticating. On a single-user appliance that is inside the threat model and is
// the price of the grant working at all; on a box with a second account it is a
// widening, and the answer there is to match `subject.user` instead - which the
// provision copy could do (it knows $TVBOX_USER) but the image copy cannot, since
// the imager picks the name.
//
// KEEP IN SYNC with the heredoc in deploy/provision.sh.
polkit.addRule(function (action, subject) {
  if (
    (action.id === "org.freedesktop.login1.reboot" || action.id === "org.freedesktop.login1.power-off") &&
    subject.isInGroup("video")
  ) {
    return polkit.Result.YES;
  }
});
